# Experiment 2.1: Is Graph Bipartite?

 1. Aim
To determine whether a given undirected graph is bipartite using Breadth First Search (BFS). A graph is bipartite if its vertices can be divided into two sets such that no two adjacent vertices belong to the same set.

 2. Software Requirements
- Operating System: Windows 10/11 or Linux
- Programming Language: C++
- IDE: VS Code / CodeBlocks / Online Compiler

 3. Test Cases

**Test Case 1**

Input:
```text
graph = [[1,3],[0,2],[1,3],[0,2]]
```

Output:
```text
true
```

Explanation: The graph can be divided into two sets without any adjacent vertices belonging to the same set.

**Test Case 2**

Input:
```text
graph = [[1,2,3],[0,2],[0,1,3],[0,2]]
```

Output:
```text
false
```

Explanation: The graph contains an odd-length cycle, so it is not bipartite.

 4. Algorithm
1. Initialize a color array with -1 for all vertices.
2. Traverse every vertex of the graph.
3. If a vertex is uncolored, start BFS from that vertex.
4. Assign color 0 to the starting vertex.
5. Visit all adjacent vertices.
6. Assign the opposite color to uncolored adjacent vertices.
7. If two adjacent vertices have the same color, return false.
8. If all vertices are colored successfully, return true.

 5. Pseudocode

```text
START
INPUT graph
INITIALIZE color array with -1

FOR each vertex i
    IF color[i] == -1
        SET color[i] = 0
        PUSH i into queue

        WHILE queue is not empty
            REMOVE vertex u from queue

            FOR each adjacent vertex v
                IF color[v] == -1
                    color[v] = 1 - color[u]
                    PUSH v into queue
                ELSE IF color[v] == color[u]
                    RETURN false

RETURN true
END
```

 6. C++ Code

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <queue>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    bool isBipartite(vector<vector<int>>& graph) {
        int n = graph.size();

        vector<int> color(n, -1);

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            if (color[i] == -1) {
                queue<int> q;

                q.push(i);
                color[i] = 0;

                while (!q.empty()) {
                    int node = q.front();
                    q.pop();

                    for (int adj : graph[node]) {
                        if (color[adj] == -1) {
                            color[adj] = 1 - color[node];
                            q.push(adj);
                        }
                        else if (color[adj] == color[node]) {
                            return false;
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        return true;
    }
};

int main() {
    vector<vector<int>> graph = {
        {1, 3},
        {0, 2},
        {1, 3},
        {0, 2}
    };

    Solution s;

    if (s.isBipartite(graph))
        cout << "true" << endl;
    else
        cout << "false" << endl;

    return 0;
}
```

 7. Output

```text
true
```

 8. Complexity Analysis
- Time Complexity: O(V + E)
- Space Complexity: O(V)

Where V represents the number of vertices and E represents the number of edges.

 9. Learning Outcomes
- Understood the concept of graphs.
- Learned the implementation of BFS.
- Understood graph coloring techniques.
- Learned how to identify bipartite graphs.
- Improved graph-based problem-solving skills.

 10. Conclusion
The Is Graph Bipartite problem was solved using BFS and graph coloring. Each vertex was assigned one of two colors, ensuring that no two adjacent vertices had the same color.

